Configuring the Nginx reverse proxy
The last thing we need to do is to configure the Nginx HTTP server to act as a reverse
proxy for our Kestrel service. Observe the following steps:
Type the following command to create a dedicated Nginx configuration file 1.
for this job:
$ sudo nano /etc/nginx/nginx-worldcities.conf
Then, fill the new file's content with the following configuration settings: 2.
server {
listen 443 ssl http2;
listen [::]:443 ssl http2;
ssl_certificate /var/ssl/worldcities.crt;
ssl_certificate_key /var/ssl/worldcities.key;
server_name worldcities.io;
root /var/www/WorldCities/;
index index.html;
autoindex off;
location / {
proxy_pass http://localhost:5000;
proxy_http_version 1.1;
proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
proxy_set_header Connection keep-alive;
proxy_set_header Host $host;
proxy_cache_bypass $http_upgrade;
proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For
$proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
}
}
Once done, press Ctrl + X to exit and then Y to save the file 3.
Right after that, execute the following command to authorize the Nginx 4.
service to connect to the network:
$ sudo setsebool -P httpd_can_network_connect 1
